{"graph_chars": "Savers face two decisions: how much to save, and in which vehicle. Economists call the first intertemporal decision making: consume now or later. Workers save for retirement because income today exceeds needs, and the reverse will be true later. If Social Security reduces private saving, it shifts the supply of financial capital left. College students are the other side: income is low today, so they borrow, then repay after they work. When consumers and firms are confident they can repay, demand for financial capital shifts right — the late nineteen nineties technology boom. In the two thousand eight and two thousand nine Great Recession, that demand shifted left. Across investments, return is a plus and risk is a minus. If Investment A gets riskier or its return falls, savers move to B: supply left for A, right for B.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0631], [0.0631, 0.1262], [0.1262, 0.1893], [0.1893, 0.2524], [0.2524, 0.3155], [0.3155, 0.3786], [0.3786, 0.4417], [0.4417, 0.5048], [0.5048, 0.5679], [0.5679, 0.631], [0.631, 0.6941], [0.6941, 0.7572], [0.7572, 0.8203], [0.8203, 0.8833], [0.8833, 0.9464], [0.9464, 1.0095], [1.0095, 1.0726], [1.0726, 1.1357], [1.1357, 1.1988], [1.1988, 1.2619], [1.2619, 1.325], [1.325, 1.3881], [1.3881, 1.4512], [1.4512, 1.5143], [1.5143, 1.5774], [1.5774, 1.6405], [1.6405, 1.7036], [1.7036, 1.7667], [1.7667, 1.8298], [1.8298, 1.8929], [1.8929, 1.956], [1.956, 2.0191], [2.0191, 2.0822], [2.0822, 2.1453], [2.1453, 2.2084], [2.2084, 2.2715], [2.2715, 2.3346], [2.3346, 2.3977], [2.3977, 2.4608], [2.4608, 2.5239], [2.5239, 2.587], [2.587, 2.65], [2.65, 2.7131], [2.7131, 2.7762], [2.7762, 2.8393], [2.8393, 2.9024], [2.9024, 2.9655], [2.9655, 3.0286], [3.0286, 3.0917], [3.0917, 3.1548], [3.1548, 3.2179], [3.2179, 3.281], [3.281, 3.3441], [3.3441, 3.4072], [3.4072, 3.4703], [3.4703, 3.5334], [3.5334, 3.5965], [3.5965, 3.6596], [3.6596, 3.7227], [3.7227, 3.7858], [3.7858, 3.8489], [3.8489, 3.912], [3.912, 3.9751], [3.9751, 4.0382], [